<div class="container masterplan">

<h1 id="top">Hardware Monitor &amp; Watchdog Plan</h1>

<div class="last-updated" style="color: var(--text-muted-color); font-size: var(--font-size-small);">
    Project: <a href="[% c.uri_for('/admin/project') %]/258">Hardware Monitor &amp; Watchdog (HWMON, #258)</a>
    &nbsp;|&nbsp; Parent: <a href="[% c.uri_for('/admin/project') %]/1">Catalyst2 application (#1)</a>
    &nbsp;|&nbsp; Status: IN-PROCESS
</div>

<p>This plan records the root-cause analysis and fix for the
<strong>hardware_monitor watchdog false CRITICAL</strong> (error todo #2065:
<code>node=workstationStandalone missed monitoring window (age=1000000000s, timeout=600)</code>),
and the remaining work to roll the fix out to the production containers.</p>

<div class="notice">
    <strong>Key finding:</strong> <code>age=1000000000s</code> is a hard-coded <em>sentinel</em> in the watchdog
    (HardwareMonitor.pm), not a real 1-billion-second gap. It means &ldquo;no <code>monitor_heartbeat</code> row
    was found for this node,&rdquo; so the watchdog had no <code>last_run</code> to compute an age from. The
    underlying cause was that the heartbeat row was <em>never being written</em>.
</div>

<h2 id="root-cause">1. Root cause (3 distinct bugs)</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Schema overflow.</strong> <code>HardwareMetrics.metric_value</code> was declared
        <code>decimal(12,3)</code> (max 999,999,999). The <code>monitor_heartbeat</code> row stores
        <code>time()</code> (epoch seconds, ~1.75e9), which overflowed the column. The <code>INSERT</code>
        failed silently inside an <code>eval</code> in <code>run</code>, so no heartbeat row was ever written.</li>
    <li><strong>Node-identity mismatch.</strong> <code>run</code> and <code>watchdog</code> derived the node key
        via <code>get_system_identifier()</code>, which appends a volatile runtime tag
        (<code> (Standalone)</code>/<code> (Docker)</code>) and the listening port non-deterministically per
        request. The two endpoints computed <em>different</em> keys, and the watchdog also filtered on a
        separately-sanitized <code>hostname</code> column &mdash; so it could never match the row <code>run</code> wrote.</li>
    <li><strong>Live DB target.</strong> The dev server resolves <code>DBEncy</code> to the <strong>PRIMARY Ency
        at 192.168.1.20:3307</strong> (not localhost:3306). The <code>decimal(15,3)</code> widening had to be
        deployed there, not on a dev-only database.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="fix">2. Fix applied (verified on :3001, 2026-08-13)</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Widened <code>HardwareMetrics.metric_value</code> to <code>decimal(15,3)</code> in the source-of-truth
        Result class (<code>Model/Schema/Ency/Result/HardwareMetrics.pm</code>) and deployed the column change to
        the PRIMARY Ency (192.168.1.20:3307) via DBIC &mdash; never hand DDL. Verified a 1.7e9 epoch value now
        stores cleanly.</li>
    <li>Added a shared <code>_node_identity()</code> helper (HardwareMonitor.pm) that strips the volatile runtime
        tag and trailing <code>:port</code>, giving both <code>run</code> (writes) and <code>watchdog</code>
        (queries) one deterministic key (<code>workstation</code>). The watchdog now matches on
        <code>system_identifier</code> alone.</li>
    <li>Fixed the host cron: replaced two broken lines (one pointed at a non-existent
        <code>/usr/local/bin/hardware_monitor.pl</code> with <code>MISSING_TOKEN</code>; the other used
        script-default nodes with no token) with one correct marked line that triggers
        <code>http://127.0.0.1:3001/admin/hardware_monitor/run</code> first and reads the token from the NFS
        share. Also fixed <code>script/hardware_monitor.pl</code> itself: its multi-line backtick <code>curl</code>
        broke under <code>/bin/sh</code> (dash, what cron uses) &mdash; rewrote as a single-line command &mdash; and
        moved the log to <code>/tmp/comserv-hardware-monitor.log</code> (the previous
        <code>/var/log/...</code> path was root-owned and unwritable by the cron user, so the job silently did
        nothing).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>POST /admin/hardware_monitor/run</code> on :3001 &rarr; HTTP 200 and writes a
<code>monitor_heartbeat</code> row to 192.168.1.20:3307; <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/watchdog</code> &rarr;
<code>ok:1, missed:0, age_sec:&lt;real seconds&gt;, last_run:&lt;epoch&gt;</code> instead of
<code>age=1000000000s</code>. The cron command runs clean under <code>dash</code> (exit 0).</p>

<h2 id="remaining">3. Current status (2026-09-19)</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>device_agent.sh deployed to all 5 monitoring targets:</strong>
        workstation (.199), proxmox720 (.2), gateway/proxmoxt210 (.3),
        comserv (.198), nfs/sunfire (.175). Each runs a 5-min cron collecting
        disk, CPU, memory, temp, uptime and POSTing to the ingest endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>Old DBI scripts replaced on .2 and .198:</strong> Both were running
        ~12KB legacy scripts that tried to connect to the DB directly and generated
        cron mail errors. Replaced with the thin trigger + shared token.</li>
    <li><strong>Workstation crontab fixed:</strong> /usr/local/etc/comserv/hw_ingest_token
        seeded from NFS. Cron reads NFS token first, local fallback.</li>
    <li><strong>Watchdog verified green:</strong> Returns <code>missed:0, ok:1</code>
        with real <code>age_sec</code> values. Heartbeats writing on every
        <code>run</code> call.</li>
    <li><strong>NFS cleaned:</strong> Removed ~63G of old log archives
        (workshop 37G, app logs 17G, catalyst errors 8.9G).</li>
    <li><strong>Health controller bug closed:</strong> <code>Comserv->json->encode()</code>
        replaced with <code>encode_json()</code> in commit 2eb488568.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>What remains:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Add Server UI page (planned: form to register new hosts and generate ready-to-paste cron lines).</li>
    <li>Dashboard improvements: show known hosts even when stale with "last reported" timestamps.</li>
    <li>Centralize token management in the Add Server UI.</li>
    <li>Potentially merge the two scripts for pure-cron hosts (device_agent could also call run).</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="architecture">5. Current Architecture — Why Two Crons/Scripts</h2>

<p>The system uses <strong>two separate cron-driven scripts</strong> for a reason:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>hardware_monitor.pl</strong> (thin trigger, lives on the gateway host only):
        <ul>
            <li>Lightweight "ping" that runs every 5 min from the gateway (.1/.3 proxmoxt210).</li>
            <li>It only curls the central app nodes (currently 192.168.1.126:5000 and .199:5000) to hit <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/run</code> and <code>/admin/logging_audit/run</code>.</li>
            <li>This wakes the app so it records heartbeats (<code>monitor_heartbeat</code> etc.) and (when DB is up) collects its own local metrics via <code>HardwareAgent</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>It does NOT collect or send the gateway's own CPU/disk/mem values.</strong></li>
            <li>Updated (2026-09) to be completely silent on success to stop cron email spam. Only real outages still produce STDERR (which can mail).</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>device_agent.sh</strong> (the actual collector — put this on any hardware):
        <ul>
            <li>Runs on the target machine (gateway, proxmox hosts, DB server, workstation, future equipment).</li>
            <li>Collects local system values (disk_used_pct_*, cpu_load_pct, mem_used_pct, temps, uptime, etc.).</li>
            <li>POSTs them as JSON to the app's <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/ingest</code> (token-protected).</li>
            <li>This is what populates <code>hardware_metrics</code> for history, dashboards, and alerts (e.g. disk space warnings).</li>
            <li>Script lives on NFS so one central copy; each host runs it from its own mount.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Why two?</strong> The gateway is an external "watchdog" host (no full app containers). It needs a tiny trigger to keep central monitoring alive. Any arbitrary hardware needs a collector to send its own numbers back. They serve different roles. On pure app servers we rely on the triggered internal collection (not device_agent) to avoid duplicates.</p>

<h3>File / Cron / Log / DB Locations</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Source (repo)</strong>: <code>Comserv/script/hardware_monitor.pl</code> and <code>script/device_agent.sh</code></li>
    <li><strong>Trigger script on gateway</strong>: copied to <code>/usr/local/bin/hardware_monitor.pl</code> (manual or via deploy on hosts that run it). Cron in host crontab (marked <code># comserv-hardware-monitor</code>). Log: <code>/tmp/comserv-hardware-monitor.log</code> (writable, avoids /var/log perms).</li>
    <li><strong>Collector (device_agent)</strong>: on NFS server (192.168.1.175) at <code>/mnt/data/comserv/scripts/device_agent.sh</code>. On each host: mounted as <code>/mnt/nfs_data/comserv/scripts/device_agent.sh</code>. Cron: manual <code>/etc/cron.d/comserv_agent</code> or crontab entry (every 5 min, with INGEST_URL + INGEST_TOKEN).</li>
    <li><strong>Deploy involvement</strong>: <code>script/deploy.sh</code> handles copying the trigger script and installing the marked cron line on hosts that run full deploy. Gateway (pure cron host) usually requires manual copy of the .pl because no app code/deploy runs there.</li>
    <li><strong>DB</strong>: <code>Ency.hardware_metrics</code> table (primary currently ~192.168.1.20:3307). Rows use <code>hostname</code> + <code>system_identifier</code> (e.g. "hostname:agent").</li>
    <li><strong>App endpoints</strong>: <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/run</code> (trigger), <code>/ingest</code> (agent posts), <code>/watchdog</code>, <code>/disk_health</code> etc. (see <code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Admin/HardwareMonitor.pm</code> and <code>Util/HardwareAgent.pm</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Currently Monitored Devices (as of 2026-09-19)</h3>
<p>The dashboard (<code>/admin/hardware_monitor</code>) shows all hosts with recent rows in <code>hardware_metrics</code> (default 2h window). All 5 targets below are actively reporting via <code>device_agent.sh</code> every 5 minutes.</p>

<table class="doc-table">
<tr class="doc-th"><th>Device</th><th>IP</th><th>Role</th><th>Scripts</th><th>SSH user</th></tr>
<tr><td>workstation</td><td>.199</td><td>Dev server + Docker host</td><td>hw_monitor.pl (root) + device_agent.sh</td><td>root</td></tr>
<tr><td>comserv</td><td>.198</td><td>Legacy DB server / host</td><td>hw_monitor.pl + device_agent.sh</td><td>shanta</td></tr>
<tr><td>proxmox720</td><td>.2</td><td>Proxmox hypervisor</td><td>hw_monitor.pl + device_agent.sh</td><td>root</td></tr>
<tr><td>gateway (proxmoxt210)</td><td>.3</td><td>Gateway / cron host</td><td>hw_monitor.pl + device_agent.sh</td><td>root</td></tr>
<tr><td>nfs (sunfire)</td><td>.175</td><td>NFS file server</td><td>device_agent.sh</td><td>shanta</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>App nodes (trigger targets)</h3>
<p>The <code>hardware_monitor.pl</code> thin trigger on each host curls these app instances to call <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/run</code>:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>production1</strong> — 192.168.1.126:5000 (Docker container)</li>
    <li><strong>workstation Docker</strong> — 192.168.1.199:5000 (Docker container)</li>
</ul>
<p>Both return HTTP 200 with the shared token. The <code>run</code> endpoint writes <code>monitor_heartbeat</code> rows for the bare hostname (<code>workstation</code>, <code>production1</code>) via the <code>_node_identity()</code> helper.</p>

<h3>db_ency (primary Ency)</h3>
<p>All hardware metrics are stored in <code>Ency.hardware_metrics</code> on the PRIMARY Ency at 192.168.1.20:3307. Rows use <code>hostname</code> for the device name and <code>system_identifier</code> for the ingress path. Query via the dashboard at <code>/admin/hardware_monitor</code>.</p>

<h2 id="expansion">6. Plans — Get All Equipment Reporting + Add Server Page</h2>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Deploy collector everywhere</strong>: Install NFS mount + <code>device_agent.sh</code> cron on every proxmox host, the gateway, DB server, any other servers/switches/appliances that can run a simple bash+curl cron. Update the reference table in <code>root/admin/HardwareMonitor/index.tt</code> and docs as hosts come online.</li>
    <li><strong>Add Server UI</strong> (planned):
        <ul>
            <li>New page or section under <code>/admin/hardware_monitor</code> (or a dedicated "Add Monitored Host").</li>
            <li>Form: hostname, IP, type (gateway, proxmox, server, etc.), optional notes.</li>
            <li>Generates the exact ready-to-paste cron line (with correct INGEST_URL for the production app and the shared token).</li>
            <li>Tracks "expected" hosts vs "last seen" from the DB.</li>
            <li>Button to "mark as deployed" or auto-detect via a one-time test POST.</li>
            <li>Future: integrate with network discovery or inventory to suggest new hosts.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Dashboard improvements</strong>: Show all known/expected hosts (even if stale) with "last reported" and "missing" badges. Better grouping by site/rack/role.</li>
    <li><strong>Central token &amp; URL management</strong>: Already partially done via env + NFS token file. Make the Add Server page pull live values from the running app config.</li>
    <li><strong>One-script simplification option</strong> (if desired): Enhance the gateway version of hardware_monitor.pl to also run the local collection + ingest before/after the trigger pings. This would mean only one script to manage on external/pure-cron hosts.</li>
</ol>

<p>Once device_agent is on all equipment, the dashboard will populate with full history and the disk/health alerts will cover everything.</p>

